														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1476120">
									

<p>I believe this is a case of bad wording. The impression I get from the rest of the article is that you <em>mean</em> that once they're out of the corridor, they stop being afraid that the corridor is a source of immediate danger. What the phrasing of that part <em>says</em>, however, is that they &quot;lose all sense of fear.&quot; What that suggests &#8212; and how I think Roget is reading it, though I don't want to put words in his mouth &#8212; is that they lose the ability to feel fear of <em>anything</em>, permanently. Try this phrasing:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In most subjects, the feelings of fear and anxiety subside immediately upon exiting the corridor.</p>
</blockquote>

